{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Anti-Eviction Act statutory grounds and notice periods","requirement":"A landlord may end a tenancy only on a statutory ground, such as nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage, lease breach, conversion, or retiring the property from residential use, after serving the required notice; some grounds require relocation assistance before eviction.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to most residential rentals: single-family homes, mobile homes and mobile home park land, apartment buildings, complexes, and rooming and boarding houses.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied premises of two or three units with two or fewer rental units","unit_limit":3},{"type":"other","text":"does not apply to a unit held in trust for a developmentally disabled immediate family member who permanently occupies it"}],"per_tenancy":"Coverage turns on the statutory ground invoked and the tenant's conduct, e.g. nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct, damage or lease breach; some grounds require relocation assistance before removal.","coverage_quotes":["The Anti-Eviction Act applies to most residential rental properties including single-family homes, mobile homes and land in a mobile home park, apartment buildings, and complexes.","This law may not apply to two - or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two (2) or fewer rental units."]},"exemptions":"Two- or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two or fewer rental units; hotel, motel and transient or seasonal guest-house guests; units held in trust for a developmentally disabled immediate family member who occupies the unit.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three months before filing a suit for eviction. The tenant can’t be evicted until relocation assistance is provided.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey ban on self-help evictions and lockouts","requirement":"A landlord may not evict or lock out a tenant without a court judgment or warrant: no padlocking, utility disconnection or blocking entry, and only a court officer may physically evict after a warrant for removal.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to tenants of New Jersey residential rental dwellings; self-help eviction is not permitted under any circumstances.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies whenever a landlord or someone acting for the landlord removes, locks out or blocks a tenant without a court judgment or warrant for removal.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"The landlord may be charged with disorderly conduct; the tenant may sue for possession and all damages, including court costs and reasonable attorney fees, and for unlawful holding of belongings.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:39-1","quoted_span":"A landlord or their agent may not padlock, disconnect utilities or otherwise block entry to a rental premise while the tenant still lives there.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
